WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:03.169
I think it was just like after
I had done the first project

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.939
and I saw the results of
I wasn't showing anyone.

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:07.507
It was just for myself.

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:08.375
Like it was like a high.

00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:30.430
And I was like,
I want to keep figuring this out as I go.

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:36.569
In 2017,

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:39.939
a 23 year
old designer in Boston had an idea.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.877
Nicole McLaughlin had taken an entry level
design job at Reebok.

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:47.380
But exposure to the waste inherent
in the manufacturing of apparel

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:51.184
inspired her to embark on a series of art
projects in her off hours.

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:55.155
She crafted Clever,
one of one footwear out of found objects

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:57.590
like tennis balls and badminton
paraphernalia.

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:01.728
Her work soon gained a cult following,
and as her audience exploded on

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:06.032
social media, she started her own company,
centered on art and upcycling,

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:08.835
teaching workshops and partnering
with blue chip brands

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:12.038
on a variety of environmentally
conscious design forward

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:15.675
projects, including collaborations
with Reebok and Vans.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.414
And it all started with one idea.

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:25.518
How did your parents

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:29.022
professional life
inform your career ambitions?

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:32.859
My parents
are both pretty creative people,

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:36.396
so I feel like I was always around it
my whole life.

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:40.233
My dad was a carpenter
and then he ended up

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:44.337
becoming a mall manager,
so he was sort of around fashion.

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:49.943
And then my mom has always been an
interior designer for commercial spaces.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:53.346
So from like my earliest memories,
there was always

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:57.417
carpet samples and like chairs in the back
seat of the car.

00:01:57.417 --> 00:02:00.587
And there was always something
creative happening in my house.

00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:05.325
Like they were very crafty and also
really good at working with what they had.

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:07.093
So I feel like that definitely

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:11.131
has influenced a lot of the decisions
that I make as a designer.

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:15.001
When would you say that you sort of fell
in love with graphic design?

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:18.138
I got a MacBook when I was in high school

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:23.510
and I got a cracked version of Illustrator
in Photoshop, and that's really

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.213
when I started doing editing
and doing some stuff on Illustrator.

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:29.682
But I think once I got to college,

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:33.052
that's really when I started
to learn more about graphic design

00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:37.257
and that I realized also
that it could be done for clothing too.

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:42.162
Who were the designers or
what were the sort of brands or products

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:45.932
that were most influential on you
in that high school period?

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:48.668
My fashion sense was pretty interesting.

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:52.338
I was more into like skateboarding
and snowboarding,

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:55.441
so I wore a lot of vans
and I loved Burton.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:59.312
And then also during high school,
I was really into sign language.

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:03.316
So I had a deaf relationship
when I was in high school

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:08.454
and I spent a lot of time on YouTube
like watching videos of people signing.

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:10.890
And so YouTube was really helpful.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.492
I wasn't the best student.

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:16.729
So I think I learned a lot from other
people or watching other people do stuff.

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:20.833
And that definitely still applies today.

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:25.905
I get creative blocks a lot
and I find that

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:29.542
the best way to get through
it is to just continue to create.

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:33.846
I find that sometimes it's helpful
if I paint something or I take photos,

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.851
you know, taking a step back
and doing something else that's creative.

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:42.355
What do you feel like was

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:45.858
like the most important things
that you learned in college?

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:51.097
It allowed me some time to grow and
to meet people and try different things.

00:03:51.097 --> 00:03:54.767
And I landed in digital media
and it was a pretty broad

00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:58.171
major
that allowed you to just kind of explore

00:03:58.171 --> 00:04:02.342
all different forms of doing photography,
doing videography, sound recording.

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:03.876
And I really appreciated that time.

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:07.780
And there was also a lot of equipment
that I could use for that for free.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.849
But essentially to me, for free,
I was like, Oh,

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:11.251
I can use all this equipment.

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:13.052
And I really took advantage of that.

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:14.220
And that's how I learned

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.590
all the programs
and things I needed for my time at Reebok.

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:22.862
Did you have any sort of sense of what you
wanted to do as a career at that point?

00:04:23.263 --> 00:04:26.899
I always was like tinkering
and using my hands to make stuff,

00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:30.937
but I never would have thought that
what I do now is even a possibility.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.641
Like, I knew the fashion industry existed
and I liked fashion,

00:04:34.641 --> 00:04:38.211
but I didn't really understand that
as like a career choice, especially with

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:42.482
footwear and graphics
being applied to garments or to footwear.

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:47.920
So I found this internship working
for Reebok, and I just applied to it.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.590
I had no idea,
like I thought I'd be doing graphics for

00:04:51.257 --> 00:04:54.627
social media or graphics
that were meant for advertisements.

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:58.197
And then once I got there, I learned that
I was doing graphics for clothing,

00:04:58.598 --> 00:05:02.468
and that was like the craziest experience
to learn how the fashion

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:05.171
industry works and understand
how close are made.

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:07.307
Once I learned all those things,
I was like,

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:10.310
This is definitely more of the direction
that I want to go in.

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:11.911
When you

00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:15.214
applied for the internship at Reebok,
was this a paid internship?

00:05:15.248 --> 00:05:19.218
It was paid, which was great, and I got
I moved to Boston, so

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:24.490
that was a really big change from college
to then moving to a new city.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.393
It was all really exciting,
but really nerve racking, and I knew

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:31.664
it was kind of my chance to prove myself
and like get into the industry.

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:34.167
So I didn't waste a second of it.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.703
And I worked really hard that whole year.

00:05:36.703 --> 00:05:38.705
And what kind of stuff did
they have you making?

00:05:39.038 --> 00:05:41.107
I was doing graphics for running.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.210
That was what I was place
on, so I was doing repeat patterns

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:48.481
for tights, for shoes
and then I got put on this project,

00:05:48.481 --> 00:05:52.251
which at the time I didn't even realize
like how much this would impact me.

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:57.957
But Reebok started doing collaborations
with some fashion brands,

00:05:57.957 --> 00:06:01.327
and that mall at the time
was just kind of taking off.

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:04.697
And they did a club with Reebok
on an in-store pump theory,

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:10.269
so I had to take this shoe
that Donna essentially scribbled all over,

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:15.108
had to take it apart and recreate it
all in Photoshop to be able

00:06:15.108 --> 00:06:19.512
to make it factory ready, and so that
they could make it in mass production.

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:23.383
And it took me weeks and weeks and weeks,
and I was like sleeping

00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:28.921
at the office and stuff and yeah,
that's kind of where it all started.

00:06:29.222 --> 00:06:34.827
I like the idea of work and I think
a lot of the people in this industry

00:06:35.161 --> 00:06:40.199
say that luck is somehow
a factor in their success, whether it's

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:45.071
being at the right place at the right time
or discovering something at that moment.

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:49.542
But there's also just the layer
of being perceptive to things

00:06:49.542 --> 00:06:54.447
and pushing yourself
to do the best you can do.

00:06:56.649 --> 00:06:58.050
How does one transition from

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:02.488
this sort of one year internship program
to becoming a full time employee there?

00:07:02.822 --> 00:07:05.491
It was kind of crazy because
they put us all against each other.

00:07:05.491 --> 00:07:08.761
It was like all your friends
that you sat next to for a whole year.

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:09.996
They're like, Get to know each other.

00:07:09.996 --> 00:07:11.631
And then they're like,
there's like one job.

00:07:11.631 --> 00:07:13.599
So you all have to fight for it now.

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:17.270
And I think my competitive spirit
really came out and I was like,

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.038
I'm going to get this job

00:07:19.038 --> 00:07:21.908
because I'm not moving back home
with my parents in New Jersey.

00:07:21.908 --> 00:07:24.210
So I used that experience

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:28.080
and I built my portfolio
and I was thankful I got the job.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.185
And so I was hired full time
doing graphics for Reebok Classics,

00:07:32.185 --> 00:07:36.422
which was where I wanted to be
because it was all the really cool,

00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:37.523
exciting products

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:41.694
that had the collaborations,
but also referenced all the archive stuff.

00:07:41.694 --> 00:07:44.063
And Reebok has an amazing history.

00:07:44.063 --> 00:07:45.698
It was really cool
to have that experience.

00:07:45.698 --> 00:07:46.833
I got to do some cool stuff.

00:07:46.833 --> 00:07:50.636
I got to do like throwback
Allen Iverson jerseys

00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:54.373
and make all the cool graphics and stuff
for inline

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:56.843
apparel.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:00.413
At what point in this journey
did you start working

00:08:00.413 --> 00:08:03.382
on your own personal art projects?

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:07.253
So I had been working at Reebok
a couple years before

00:08:07.253 --> 00:08:10.356
I started doing my personal projects.

00:08:10.356 --> 00:08:15.161
I started to realize how much waste
there was coming out of the industry.

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:18.798
I would be sitting at my desk
and there'd be boxes of samples

00:08:18.798 --> 00:08:22.335
just surrounding me,
and it's a half a pair.

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:24.670
That's the wrong color
or the wrong material.

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:28.541
So it kind of is just destined
to be trashed and thrown out.

00:08:28.908 --> 00:08:33.079
And so I started to rummage and pick
through these things that they were

00:08:33.079 --> 00:08:35.715
just going to get rid of
and taking them home with me, like

00:08:35.715 --> 00:08:37.450
not really knowing what I was going to do,
but I was like,

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:39.452
I could really try to find a way
to use this.

00:08:39.452 --> 00:08:42.121
I first started gluing everything,
and then from there

00:08:42.121 --> 00:08:43.589
I was like all of a handsaw.

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:46.192
And then I got better every project.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:50.196
When I say where my ideas

00:08:50.196 --> 00:08:54.100
come from, it sounds a little bit corny,
but they really do come from everywhere.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:58.404
I think a lot of the time material is used
only for its intended purpose,

00:08:58.404 --> 00:09:02.842
so I like to try to change that.

00:09:07.246 --> 00:09:09.482
You're a young person in a new city.

00:09:09.482 --> 00:09:12.351
I imagine
you are pursuing an active social life

00:09:12.351 --> 00:09:15.254
and you have other things going on
and you're also working your ass off at

00:09:15.955 --> 00:09:16.889
your job.

00:09:16.889 --> 00:09:21.127
Where's the motivation coming from
to be like, Oh, I'm going to spend like

00:09:21.193 --> 00:09:24.497
12 hours at home tinkering with this thing

00:09:24.497 --> 00:09:26.499
or teaching myself to sew without

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:29.769
a concrete end in mind.

00:09:29.769 --> 00:09:32.138
I look back at that time and

00:09:32.738 --> 00:09:36.142
I, I think it was just like after
I had done

00:09:36.142 --> 00:09:39.879
the first project and I saw the results of
I wasn't showing anyone.

00:09:39.879 --> 00:09:41.013
It was just for myself.

00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:45.718
It made me feel really good and inspired
and I was like, I want to keep.

00:09:45.751 --> 00:09:46.586
Like, it was like a high.

00:09:46.586 --> 00:09:48.387
And I was like,
I want to keep figuring this out.

00:09:48.387 --> 00:09:49.855
Like, what if I get better?

00:09:49.855 --> 00:09:52.858
And like,
what if I just keep taking new materials

00:09:52.858 --> 00:09:56.395
and figuring this out
as I go on the weekends?

00:09:56.395 --> 00:09:56.996
I would go out

00:09:56.996 --> 00:10:01.701
and try to rummage through thrift stores
and my house and everywhere.

00:10:01.701 --> 00:10:06.706
Reebok had an office in Canton that
they closed, and I would go back there and

00:10:07.473 --> 00:10:10.376
try to find materials
and take it home with me.

00:10:10.509 --> 00:10:13.045
That energy was
just so high at the time to

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:20.252
How long
did it take from sort of starting to like,

00:10:20.252 --> 00:10:24.857
acquire these skills to getting to a place
where you're even showing friends?

00:10:25.157 --> 00:10:27.259
I'd say you're probably

00:10:27.259 --> 00:10:30.997
I was making this stuff
just in my bedroom, not showing anybody.

00:10:30.997 --> 00:10:35.868
And then I would show like some friends
and then I would be working

00:10:35.868 --> 00:10:40.840
on stuff at my desk occasionally because
I was like, starting to take over my life.

00:10:40.973 --> 00:10:42.708
It was like
the only thing I was thinking of.

00:10:42.708 --> 00:10:45.111
And then I started posting on Social

00:10:45.611 --> 00:10:49.115
and one of my first ones
that I felt good about that I posted it.

00:10:49.348 --> 00:10:51.851
Someone was like,
Oh, was this an actual shoe?

00:10:52.318 --> 00:10:54.787
And I was like,
Oh, that's like such a crazy complex.

00:10:54.787 --> 00:10:56.889
I went from hot gluing something
to making something

00:10:56.889 --> 00:10:58.891
that looked like
it could actually have been made.

00:10:58.891 --> 00:11:02.662
And so from then I was just like,
Oh, I can just keep showing this

00:11:02.662 --> 00:11:04.797
and show the progress of me making stuff.

00:11:04.797 --> 00:11:09.969
And then a couple of months after that,
I made a series where I did a shoe out

00:11:09.969 --> 00:11:13.873
of a tennis ball, shoe out of a volleyball
and a shoe out of a soccer ball.

00:11:13.873 --> 00:11:18.611
And that's when I started to like, pick up
traction on like social and followers.

00:11:18.611 --> 00:11:20.446
And it was really scary.

00:11:20.446 --> 00:11:23.582
Honestly, I was not prepared for it
and I didn't realize how fast

00:11:23.582 --> 00:11:24.450
that could happen.

00:11:25.951 --> 00:11:26.719
It was exciting.

00:11:26.719 --> 00:11:30.790
But also I was like, Do I have what
it takes to continue doing this?

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:34.293
And like, am I doing it for me
or am I doing it for them now?

00:11:34.527 --> 00:11:37.697
This was always a creative side project
and I never wanted to lose that.

00:11:38.097 --> 00:11:40.599
It was a really fast switch.

00:11:40.599 --> 00:11:43.135
Like once
all the traction started, I was like,

00:11:43.135 --> 00:11:47.540
Oh wow, I have to be taking this series.

00:11:48.974 --> 00:11:51.277
After
getting her first taste of public success.

00:11:51.444 --> 00:11:53.779
Nicole
continued to expand on her tradecraft,

00:11:53.979 --> 00:11:57.483
including honing her skills
at the legendary Adidas Creator's Farm

00:11:57.883 --> 00:12:00.820
as her unique passion
projects continued to attract attention.

00:12:01.053 --> 00:12:03.723
She recognized an opportunity
to step out on her own

00:12:03.956 --> 00:12:07.860
and found a company that could encompass
the breadth of her upcycling ambition,

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:12.431
from collabs to consulting to education.

00:12:17.403 --> 00:12:18.571
During your time at Reebok,

00:12:18.571 --> 00:12:21.874
you ended up going to work at the farm

00:12:22.041 --> 00:12:24.610
in Adidas, the parent company of Reebok

00:12:25.411 --> 00:12:28.581
work with Marc Dolce,
who was, you know, sort of a design guide.

00:12:28.881 --> 00:12:31.650
What was that experience like
and how was it different

00:12:32.251 --> 00:12:34.620
than the experience within Reebok?

00:12:34.754 --> 00:12:38.457
The farm was this Disneyland
for designers.

00:12:38.791 --> 00:12:41.293
Basically,
it was an office where people could come

00:12:41.293 --> 00:12:45.464
and they had an unlimited resource
of materials

00:12:45.464 --> 00:12:48.801
and a makerspace and knowledgeable
people who worked in there.

00:12:49.168 --> 00:12:52.972
The designers from Adidas would come
and they would do a three month rotation.

00:12:53.506 --> 00:12:57.376
And I was the first Reebok designer
that got offered the opportunity

00:12:57.376 --> 00:12:59.245
to do the rotation at the farm.

00:12:59.245 --> 00:13:02.047
So I was really, really excited for that.

00:13:02.214 --> 00:13:06.452
And at the time I had just really started
doing my personal projects.

00:13:07.153 --> 00:13:10.990
I was in such a creative space,
filled with so many creative people

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:14.026
that were almost on this
like sabbatical thing.

00:13:14.059 --> 00:13:14.760
So yeah.

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:19.165
Are you working on like projects
that are meant to come to fruition

00:13:19.165 --> 00:13:23.102
or is it more sort of like a R&amp;D
like experimental thing?

00:13:23.235 --> 00:13:26.906
A bit of both, but definitely more with
the intention of like creating projects

00:13:27.072 --> 00:13:29.942
that would come out in
maybe three or four years time.

00:13:29.942 --> 00:13:32.411
It was pretty advanced like timeline.

00:13:32.678 --> 00:13:38.184
And so yeah, that was just like
a knowledge explosion during that time

00:13:38.184 --> 00:13:44.223
and selling that, that was like
where I really got my skill set.

00:13:46.091 --> 00:13:47.927
My biggest advantage as a

00:13:47.927 --> 00:13:52.231
creator is to build, to show
what goes on in my mind physically.

00:13:52.565 --> 00:13:55.201
I think it's a visual representation

00:13:55.201 --> 00:13:57.570
of my thoughts

00:13:58.204 --> 00:14:01.207
as you start to have these viral moments.

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.409
Is this something that is talked
about at Reebok

00:14:03.409 --> 00:14:06.212
with your manager, with your team? And

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:10.950
the majority of the people I worked with
had no idea that this was happening.

00:14:11.317 --> 00:14:13.752
And then there was one time

00:14:14.186 --> 00:14:17.489
and this was the moment that I knew
it was time to quit.

00:14:18.858 --> 00:14:22.561
I was in a meeting
and this outside marketing

00:14:22.595 --> 00:14:25.931
agency
came in to do a presentation to Reebok,

00:14:25.931 --> 00:14:29.768
and they put me in a deck and were like,
Oh, you should collaborate with this girl.

00:14:30.369 --> 00:14:32.972
And I was sitting in the meeting
and all my coworkers, like, turned

00:14:32.972 --> 00:14:35.207
and looked at me and I was like, Okay,
I guess

00:14:36.108 --> 00:14:38.244
I got to do something about that.

00:14:38.244 --> 00:14:40.112
I never thought that I would do freelance.

00:14:40.112 --> 00:14:42.748
I never thought I would own a business.

00:14:42.748 --> 00:14:44.316
I didn't understand anything about it.

00:14:44.316 --> 00:14:48.888
I was kind of scared actually,
to even consider that, because in my head

00:14:48.888 --> 00:14:52.691
I was like, I've made it Like I have a
like a corporate job that has like a name.

00:14:52.691 --> 00:14:57.029
And, you know, I've worked
really hard to get here, but I knew that

00:14:57.029 --> 00:15:00.666
I wasn't I'm not always going to have,
you know, the most popular social media.

00:15:00.666 --> 00:15:02.401
I'm not always going to be
the most relevant.

00:15:02.401 --> 00:15:04.303
And I'm totally fine with that.

00:15:04.303 --> 00:15:07.840
But the moments that you do have it, it's
kind of like a make or break thing where

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:12.044
it's like, if you want to use this moment,
use it and capitalize on it.

00:15:12.044 --> 00:15:14.313
And so I was like, Let's do it.

00:15:14.313 --> 00:15:15.581
Making things the way that you do.

00:15:15.581 --> 00:15:17.950
And they're all, you know,
sort of one of one's

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:20.085
or at least that, that in that moment
it was all one of ones.

00:15:20.386 --> 00:15:26.091
The obvious path towards monetizing
that would be to go and sell them

00:15:26.091 --> 00:15:29.028
and figure out whether, you know, it's on
some sort of marketplace or whatever.

00:15:29.361 --> 00:15:33.933
You were very pointed
in, not wanting to sell these pieces.

00:15:34.066 --> 00:15:37.803
What was the sort of motivation
philosophically to not have any sort

00:15:37.803 --> 00:15:40.472
of commercial element
with the personal projects?

00:15:41.006 --> 00:15:46.812
First of all, it's really challenging
to make these pieces sometimes

00:15:46.812 --> 00:15:51.283
and like at scale,
if I was to sit there and make even only

00:15:51.417 --> 00:15:53.986
ten or 20 versions of IS,

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:57.256
first of all, it's never going
to look exactly the same every time

00:15:57.256 --> 00:15:58.891
something about it,
it's going to feel different.

00:15:58.891 --> 00:16:03.729
And a lot of the time the material I use
there is only one I could find of it.

00:16:03.729 --> 00:16:06.498
And sometimes I'm using mismatched
soles or,

00:16:06.765 --> 00:16:10.936
you know, different parts of things
and it's hard to duplicate what I do.

00:16:11.537 --> 00:16:14.740
Something that I always realized
as as a consumer myself.

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:17.543
Like you see stuff online and like
immediate, you're like, Oh, I like that.

00:16:17.543 --> 00:16:18.844
I want to buy it.

00:16:18.844 --> 00:16:22.881
And I don't feel like
everything needs to be purchased.

00:16:22.881 --> 00:16:27.486
And so a lot of the pieces that I have
that are a bit like crazier, wild or

00:16:27.553 --> 00:16:31.490
like you wouldn't have the same experience
even if you did own it.

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:34.360
Like, I think it's something
about what you get when you see it.

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:38.597
You have it, you feel it, and then
you kind of keep moving on it your day.

00:16:38.597 --> 00:16:41.400
You'll come back and look at it
or reference it or however, but

00:16:41.667 --> 00:16:45.304
you don't need to own it
to have an experience with it.

00:16:47.206 --> 00:16:50.743
How you know, as you are thinking,
I'm going to quit my job

00:16:51.076 --> 00:16:55.547
and pursue running my own,
you know, myself as a business kind of.

00:16:55.948 --> 00:17:00.652
How were you thinking about monetizing it
and making it work financially?

00:17:00.786 --> 00:17:05.591
It started actually with the workshops,
so that was my first opportunity

00:17:05.591 --> 00:17:09.294
as a freelancer was like, Oh, well,
why don't you teach people how to sew?

00:17:09.294 --> 00:17:10.896
Like if that's what you know,
how to do it?

00:17:10.896 --> 00:17:14.533
And again, like I had just started this,
like I was not an expert, but I was like,

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:18.971
I can teach people if I could figure this
out, like people can do this.

00:17:19.238 --> 00:17:21.140
I had that as an opportunity.

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:24.143
And then my first like bigger
brand project was

00:17:24.143 --> 00:17:26.578
with a extension of Foot Locker.

00:17:26.612 --> 00:17:29.648
It was called Green House,
and they offered me

00:17:29.648 --> 00:17:32.918
a project
to do a collaboration with Crocs.

00:17:32.918 --> 00:17:36.088
And so I was like, okay,
like I didn't know what that really meant.

00:17:36.088 --> 00:17:37.756
And I was like, Can this be scaled

00:17:37.756 --> 00:17:41.927
in a way that still feels like me,
that still has a part of my identity

00:17:41.927 --> 00:17:46.298
in the ethos of upcycling, knowing that
it's still it's going to be created new?

00:17:46.598 --> 00:17:48.000
How can I make this me?

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:49.768
And so that's sort of where it started.

00:17:49.768 --> 00:17:52.838
And I saw a business opportunity
and then I started to realize

00:17:52.838 --> 00:17:55.507
I can work with these companies
from the inside out.

00:17:55.507 --> 00:17:59.478
So my experience from Reebok
really can apply here where it's like

00:17:59.478 --> 00:18:02.247
these brands have all this stuff
and I know it because I've seen it.

00:18:02.714 --> 00:18:04.116
How can we find a way to use it?

00:18:04.116 --> 00:18:07.019
Can we bring this back into production?
Can we do workshops?

00:18:07.052 --> 00:18:08.120
You know, there's to me

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:12.391
use dollar signs because it's material
that they're ready to throw away.

00:18:12.391 --> 00:18:12.925
And I was like,

00:18:12.925 --> 00:18:17.262
If I can make something cool out of it,
like, I'll take it and I'll, I'll use it.

00:18:17.496 --> 00:18:22.034
And then they're like, Well,
how can we get involved in it?

00:18:23.635 --> 00:18:25.671
My ultimate creative goal is

00:18:25.737 --> 00:18:29.641
to make upcycling a more popular thing,

00:18:29.641 --> 00:18:35.180
to be able to show the potential of waste
and to show that the fashion industry

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:38.550
as a whole can do a lot better.

00:18:42.654 --> 00:18:43.388
What was the process

00:18:43.388 --> 00:18:47.726
for putting together kind of the itinerary
or the curriculum for the workshops?

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:51.630
The workshops have always been more
How can someone leave

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:54.800
with a skill that they didn't have
when they came?

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:58.170
And I always thought
that sewing was really scary.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:01.106
And I like
sometimes I still think it kind of scary.

00:19:01.507 --> 00:19:06.011
And if you been in front of a sewing
machine before, it's like there's

00:19:06.011 --> 00:19:07.713
a lot of power that you hold in your hands

00:19:07.713 --> 00:19:09.815
and you're like,
I don't really know what I'm doing.

00:19:10.082 --> 00:19:11.717
I want them to leave
with the finished product.

00:19:11.717 --> 00:19:13.051
But if anything, if they can leave

00:19:13.051 --> 00:19:15.320
with some type of skill set,
even if it is hands.

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:19.591
So just to be able to understand
how something is made,

00:19:19.925 --> 00:19:22.861
I think is important
that can leave with them being able

00:19:22.861 --> 00:19:26.398
to repair something or be able to hem
a pair of pants that they've drifted.

00:19:26.865 --> 00:19:30.802
If we work with a brand, it's
a sponsored moment where they'll provide

00:19:30.802 --> 00:19:36.241
a box of shoe samples or deadstock fabric
or clothes that are donated back to them.

00:19:36.241 --> 00:19:37.709
And we'll use that.

00:19:37.709 --> 00:19:42.181
And it's cool because they get to have
the discovery element of taking something

00:19:42.181 --> 00:19:45.317
and flipping it inside out,
finding pockets, finding pollers,

00:19:45.317 --> 00:19:47.986
and using everything that you
can because it's valuable.

00:19:49.988 --> 00:19:50.489
You left

00:19:50.489 --> 00:19:53.892
Reebok in 2019 and started this business

00:19:53.892 --> 00:19:57.763
sort of in earnest,
going right into the pandemic.

00:19:57.963 --> 00:20:01.066
How did that affect
kind of getting things off the ground?

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:02.768
That was really scary.

00:20:02.768 --> 00:20:07.172
I think that was the craziest time
to decide to go freelance.

00:20:07.172 --> 00:20:09.708
I was really nervous to make that jump.

00:20:09.708 --> 00:20:13.212
And then once that happened
to, I was like, What am I going to do?

00:20:13.712 --> 00:20:19.351
And also because my well of materials
just kind of dried up

00:20:19.351 --> 00:20:24.323
because I didn't have Reebok materials
and all the thrift stores and things

00:20:24.323 --> 00:20:27.559
that I was going to to get materials
I didn't have during COVID either.

00:20:27.993 --> 00:20:31.096
So it really forced me to dig super deep.

00:20:31.263 --> 00:20:34.900
And I was just using literally
everything in my house.

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:38.604
And I was getting really creative
with the stuff that I had.

00:20:38.604 --> 00:20:41.640
And this is also around
the time that I started

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:44.643
taking apart
all the projects that I was making.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:46.511
So I was like, I'll make it.

00:20:46.511 --> 00:20:48.313
I'll take a picture of it,
I'll take it apart,

00:20:48.313 --> 00:20:50.949
and then I can use that material again
for another project.

00:20:50.949 --> 00:20:54.753
So a lot of the stuff that I've made, like
if finally seen it and you're like, Oh,

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:55.921
that's actually about the same.

00:20:55.921 --> 00:21:00.392
So like, that's the same fleece material
that's been used, you know, at this point

00:21:00.826 --> 00:21:01.827
dozens of times.

00:21:01.827 --> 00:21:06.865
So I think is a really good thing
to happen.

00:21:06.865 --> 00:21:09.401
And also started to realize

00:21:09.401 --> 00:21:13.372
I needed to create boundaries
between me and my work too.

00:21:13.405 --> 00:21:17.576
I think as a freelancer, you start
to realize, like you run a business

00:21:17.576 --> 00:21:21.446
that becomes your whole entire life
and you never able to turn it off.

00:21:22.581 --> 00:21:25.584
I was experiencing a lot of burnout
then I was like,

00:21:25.617 --> 00:21:29.521
If I don't set these healthy boundaries
now, will I ever?

00:21:29.888 --> 00:21:32.691
That was also a time of reflection
for me to really do

00:21:32.691 --> 00:21:34.926
that.

00:21:36.295 --> 00:21:39.064
Striking out on her own
just as the pandemic began,

00:21:39.064 --> 00:21:42.868
would force Nicole to expand her DIY
toolkit even further.

00:21:43.502 --> 00:21:46.571
Her technical skills and philosophy
regarding the creative process

00:21:46.705 --> 00:21:50.776
would provide a broad foundation
for success, culminating with a satisfying

00:21:50.776 --> 00:21:59.551
full circle moment.

00:22:00.719 --> 00:22:04.723
I think it's interesting just hearing
the language used that you frame it

00:22:04.723 --> 00:22:09.661
as going freelance as opposed to like
launching a brand or starting a company.

00:22:09.861 --> 00:22:12.030
Do you still feel like

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:15.033
you are like a freelance designer,
or do you feel like you are,

00:22:15.834 --> 00:22:18.770
you know, the captain of this enterprise?

00:22:19.304 --> 00:22:22.607
I've legitimized the operation,
so I should have

00:22:22.607 --> 00:22:25.711
I should graduate
from the term freelancer.

00:22:25.711 --> 00:22:29.848
But for some reason in my head,
I mentally kind of still there.

00:22:30.048 --> 00:22:33.485
I don't feel big enough,
I guess, to be a brand, but at this point,

00:22:33.485 --> 00:22:37.789
like I am creating stuff
that people are wearing and buying.

00:22:37.789 --> 00:22:40.592
So in a way,
like that's sort of how it is.

00:22:40.592 --> 00:22:45.130
And I am an LLC and I pay people
and so it is a bigger ship,

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:49.468
then I'm still thinking it's a sailboat,
I guess, and it's actually like a ship,

00:22:49.768 --> 00:22:52.904
but I like mentally kind of keeping

00:22:52.904 --> 00:22:56.007
it still as like my personal project.

00:22:56.007 --> 00:22:56.975
I don't want to lose that.

00:22:56.975 --> 00:22:58.510
I'm like scared
that I'm going to lose that

00:22:58.510 --> 00:23:02.681
because that's why I love it
so much and it's mine and I care about it

00:23:02.681 --> 00:23:07.686
and I don't want it to become so big
that it's like a machine.

00:23:07.953 --> 00:23:09.921
How would you describe your brand?

00:23:09.921 --> 00:23:15.861
I have a couple different arms of it where
my personal work is still the forefront.

00:23:15.961 --> 00:23:17.462
That's where I get my ideas.

00:23:17.462 --> 00:23:20.932
I still love dedicating my time
to making things

00:23:20.932 --> 00:23:24.169
and trying things out and knowing that
it's probably not going to work.

00:23:24.169 --> 00:23:26.304
And then I have these brand projects

00:23:26.304 --> 00:23:30.542
where it's a collaboration
and it's footwear, it's apparel.

00:23:30.542 --> 00:23:34.646
They come to me, they have an idea
for a model of a shoe, and I'm like, Okay,

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:38.884
well what material do you have
that's potentially destined for the trash?

00:23:38.884 --> 00:23:40.051
Like, can we use that?

00:23:40.051 --> 00:23:41.486
Can we do a super small run?

00:23:41.486 --> 00:23:43.321
And we can do a really like,

00:23:43.321 --> 00:23:47.092
small customizable version of it
and make it a little bit crazier.

00:23:47.192 --> 00:23:51.763
I think it's more about still, upcycling
is at the forefront of everything

00:23:51.763 --> 00:23:56.468
and making responsible design choices
when it comes to sourcing materials.

00:23:56.468 --> 00:24:01.173
But whimsy and fun is also
a big part of my friend, and my identity

00:24:01.173 --> 00:24:04.843
is playfulness
and putting pockets on things that don't

00:24:05.043 --> 00:24:09.748
maybe necessarily need a pocket,
but I think it does.

00:24:10.382 --> 00:24:14.152
What are the qualities about yourself
that you hold on to the most tightly?

00:24:14.786 --> 00:24:17.189
My childhood sense of wonder.

00:24:17.222 --> 00:24:22.327
I think that's always been
a huge part of my work and just my life.

00:24:22.327 --> 00:24:26.164
I love to have fun
and I don't always love to take things too

00:24:26.164 --> 00:24:31.770
seriously, especially being in an industry
that is pretty serious at times.

00:24:31.770 --> 00:24:35.607
I think it's always fun to approach it
with a lightheartedness,

00:24:35.607 --> 00:24:39.044
and that's what's kind of kept me going.

00:24:42.013 --> 00:24:44.783
A lot of the work you do is with brands,

00:24:45.083 --> 00:24:48.553
which means that you have to
sort of interface with people there

00:24:48.553 --> 00:24:52.991
and they have their own agenda
and their expectations that always

00:24:52.991 --> 00:24:57.696
at the outset of these relationships
are aligned, at least on paper.

00:24:57.863 --> 00:25:03.168
But then as it plays out, sometimes
there is it reveals a misalignment.

00:25:03.335 --> 00:25:05.670
Yeah, I I've definitely experience that.

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:08.940
I'd say the majority of the time
the brands that I work

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:12.410
with kind of know
what they're signing up for.

00:25:12.811 --> 00:25:16.681
And I'm not I'm not a safe designer

00:25:16.848 --> 00:25:20.318
in any aspect of the process.

00:25:20.552 --> 00:25:25.023
I like to sort of push my limits
and also the company

00:25:25.023 --> 00:25:28.693
that I'm working with limits
on what they've made in the past

00:25:28.693 --> 00:25:33.698
and what we can create together
that feels authentic to both of us.

00:25:34.165 --> 00:25:38.370
But I think having the design, integrity
and also identity

00:25:38.436 --> 00:25:41.406
for me is really important to keep secure.

00:25:41.907 --> 00:25:45.744
Brands will come to me now for advice,
which I love, and they're like,

00:25:45.877 --> 00:25:47.312
We don't know what to do
with all this stuff.

00:25:47.312 --> 00:25:49.514
We have a box
that's sitting in the warehouse.

00:25:49.514 --> 00:25:51.950
It's costing us more money
to have it in the warehouse

00:25:51.950 --> 00:25:53.151
than it would be to get rid of it.

00:25:53.151 --> 00:25:54.286
Let's call Nicole.

00:25:54.286 --> 00:25:56.288
Like, what kind of things would that be?

00:25:56.288 --> 00:25:56.955
It's crazy.

00:25:56.955 --> 00:25:59.824
Like things that don't even make it
onto the floor at a store

00:25:59.824 --> 00:26:05.030
because the tag on the clothes, like,
maybe created a small hole in it,

00:26:05.430 --> 00:26:08.600
even if, like, there was a couple pieces
like that, they'll get sent back.

00:26:08.867 --> 00:26:12.137
So like, it's
not even noticeable to a consumer or

00:26:12.137 --> 00:26:17.042
if a roll of fabric has like one
water mark or some type of like

00:26:17.042 --> 00:26:20.879
ink mark on one part of the roll,
the whole thing will get scrapped.

00:26:21.012 --> 00:26:22.180
Just like we're testing.

00:26:22.180 --> 00:26:24.282
They could be halfway
through like producing a shoe

00:26:24.282 --> 00:26:27.719
and then something fails on it
and they're like, actually, nope,

00:26:27.719 --> 00:26:30.755
we got to take everything
off the factory line and figure this out.

00:26:31.556 --> 00:26:33.391
That's a big part
of what I've been doing now.

00:26:33.391 --> 00:26:35.827
I love that type of work
and a lot of the time

00:26:35.827 --> 00:26:39.464
the brand partnerships start
as like a social media ask

00:26:39.464 --> 00:26:40.231
and then have turned

00:26:40.231 --> 00:26:43.301
into this whole like I'm like,
let me take a look at your factory

00:26:43.301 --> 00:26:45.370
and your business
and let's see what we can do here.

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:48.907
And definitely overstepping boundaries
as much as I possibly can.

00:26:49.374 --> 00:26:51.409
How do you think about the content
that you're making?

00:26:51.409 --> 00:26:52.410
And, you know,

00:26:52.410 --> 00:26:54.813
the upside of social media
is the interconnectedness

00:26:54.813 --> 00:26:56.948
and the fact that we can reach
so many people.

00:26:56.948 --> 00:27:01.653
The downside is that, yes,
you know, that you're in a feedback loop,

00:27:01.653 --> 00:27:04.556
even if you're trying to fight
being in that feedback loop.

00:27:04.689 --> 00:27:08.193
I think at some point
I had to tune it out.

00:27:08.293 --> 00:27:09.861
It's nice to be able to create

00:27:09.861 --> 00:27:13.732
a place for conversation,
and I like that and I always I leave

00:27:14.232 --> 00:27:18.003
comments are always on
because I want people to have a dialog.

00:27:18.003 --> 00:27:21.506
But at the same time,
you shouldn't let what people say

00:27:21.506 --> 00:27:24.509
always influence the decisions
that you're making.

00:27:24.509 --> 00:27:26.711
And and over time I was like,
you know what?

00:27:27.078 --> 00:27:30.181
People are going to have their opinions,
whether they like it or not.

00:27:30.348 --> 00:27:33.385
They're thinking about it,
they're commenting on it.

00:27:33.385 --> 00:27:38.023
And I think the ultimate message
that I'm trying to achieve

00:27:38.023 --> 00:27:41.159
is getting people's
perception around waste.

00:27:41.159 --> 00:27:44.562
And, you know, the
the bad side of the fashion industry.

00:27:44.562 --> 00:27:47.532
I want people to change their minds
about this stuff.

00:27:47.532 --> 00:27:51.903
And so as long as they're interacting
with it some way, it's helpful.

00:27:52.003 --> 00:27:55.840
I think.

00:27:55.840 --> 00:28:00.145
I think to be able to tell a good idea
from a bad idea, you have to do both.

00:28:00.145 --> 00:28:03.715
And I definitely have experienced
a lot of bad ideas

00:28:03.715 --> 00:28:07.052
and they somehow
always lead me in the right direction.

00:28:07.052 --> 00:28:10.922
So I just kind of have to let it happen.

00:28:14.626 --> 00:28:14.926
Has there

00:28:14.926 --> 00:28:18.730
ever been a project that you've made
and felt

00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:22.200
so good about and confident in

00:28:22.801 --> 00:28:25.437
and then posted it and had it not connect?

00:28:25.904 --> 00:28:30.542
A while ago
I had all this material from Puma

00:28:31.142 --> 00:28:35.280
and it was samples
that were made for the Olympics

00:28:35.747 --> 00:28:39.184
and it was like tracks, spikes and balls

00:28:39.184 --> 00:28:42.587
and gloves and all these like things
that were meant to be for sport.

00:28:42.954 --> 00:28:45.657
And I it was a sculptural piece

00:28:45.657 --> 00:28:49.494
and it wasn't really meant to be wearable,
but it was meant to be more for display.

00:28:49.527 --> 00:28:53.364
My idea was to make like art,
like a series of armor

00:28:53.364 --> 00:28:57.135
out of all these, like track spikes
on, like, the arms and like,

00:28:57.435 --> 00:29:01.372
I think that was definitely like
one of those times where I was like, okay,

00:29:01.406 --> 00:29:04.709
like conceptual
doesn't always hit with the audience,

00:29:04.709 --> 00:29:08.213
but to me
it really pushed my design skills.

00:29:08.213 --> 00:29:11.149
And also
I got a lot of projects out of it.

00:29:11.382 --> 00:29:16.387
I had so many people reference like brands
that came to me after that reference

00:29:16.387 --> 00:29:19.791
that that image, and they were like,
This was like a sculpture.

00:29:19.791 --> 00:29:20.992
Like we want something like this.

00:29:20.992 --> 00:29:24.062
And I ended up doing a sculpture
for the Super Bowl

00:29:24.062 --> 00:29:27.165
last year for Nike
because of that project.

00:29:27.432 --> 00:29:29.534
And it was just kind of crazy
that that happened.

00:29:29.534 --> 00:29:32.170
So I always have like people
that aren't really sure about

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:35.373
if they should post something or
if they think it's too out there, like not

00:29:35.373 --> 00:29:39.778
their normal style, like go for it anyway,
because like, who cares about metrics?

00:29:39.778 --> 00:29:41.613
At the end of the day,
if it's going to get you work

00:29:41.613 --> 00:29:45.416
and it's going to get you the right
people's attention, you never know.

00:29:45.416 --> 00:29:48.353
So it worked out.

00:29:51.523 --> 00:29:52.190
Obviously,

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:54.692
as the business has grown
and the opportunities have grown,

00:29:55.393 --> 00:30:00.231
you don't physically make every
single thing with your hands anymore.

00:30:00.231 --> 00:30:02.400
You now have people that you work with.

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:04.803
How has it been to sort of detach yourself

00:30:04.869 --> 00:30:08.973
from the physicality
of that part of the creation?

00:30:09.174 --> 00:30:10.441
It's challenging.

00:30:10.441 --> 00:30:14.913
I never thought that
I would give that part of it up,

00:30:14.913 --> 00:30:19.617
but at some point I had to
to be able to be a business person

00:30:19.617 --> 00:30:25.290
and be able to go and take meetings
and go places and and keep things running.

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:26.691
Because it used to be

00:30:26.691 --> 00:30:29.661
if I had to travel somewhere,
everything stopped and I had to.

00:30:29.894 --> 00:30:33.565
I was sewing in hotel rooms and like it,
it wasn't working for me.

00:30:33.998 --> 00:30:40.271
So I found people that I really trust
and that understand me and my vision.

00:30:40.271 --> 00:30:45.210
But also they have their own vision
as well and how we can work together.

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:48.446
And I think I still have a hand
in and everything.

00:30:48.446 --> 00:30:52.550
And whether that's the concept
is usually coming, that's me usually.

00:30:52.951 --> 00:30:55.253
And the physical making part of

00:30:55.253 --> 00:30:59.123
it could be passed off to other people,
but it's, it's ours.

00:30:59.123 --> 00:31:02.827
Like it still feels like a part of me
because it came from my brain.

00:31:02.827 --> 00:31:06.531
And, you know, just because I didn't sit
there and physically so the whole thing.

00:31:06.898 --> 00:31:09.868
But that was a big letting go moment.

00:31:09.868 --> 00:31:14.138
And I think a lot of people experience
that as a creative.

00:31:14.138 --> 00:31:17.475
But in any business sense
where it's like you were the one doing

00:31:17.475 --> 00:31:20.879
every job and at some point it's like
you can't physically do everything.

00:31:20.879 --> 00:31:22.881
So you're going
to have to be able to delegate.

00:31:22.881 --> 00:31:26.251
And I've gotten better at it,
but it's always something

00:31:26.251 --> 00:31:30.154
that I'm working on.

00:31:30.154 --> 00:31:35.326
The most important idea that I've ever had
is letting go.

00:31:35.526 --> 00:31:40.565
I think for me, my projects
are meant to live in a moment of time

00:31:40.999 --> 00:31:45.436
and I never stay stuck on one thing.

00:31:49.807 --> 00:31:51.142
You have

00:31:51.142 --> 00:31:54.545
established yourself very prominently
in the footwear industry

00:31:55.680 --> 00:31:58.583
without necessarily coming into it
as a sneakerhead

00:31:58.650 --> 00:32:02.220
or really like sneaker enthusiast.

00:32:02.220 --> 00:32:05.290
What would you say
is your sort of relationship to

00:32:06.457 --> 00:32:07.625
sneakers at this point?

00:32:07.625 --> 00:32:13.131
I do love sneakers,
and I didn't grow a sneaker head per se.

00:32:13.164 --> 00:32:16.935
I mean, I always thought
about my footwear choices,

00:32:16.935 --> 00:32:20.171
but it was always more
from a utility standpoint and it's

00:32:20.171 --> 00:32:24.309
still a lot of
the time is what has brought me closer to

00:32:24.309 --> 00:32:29.414
them is the history of shoes
and how inspiring that is to me.

00:32:29.547 --> 00:32:34.419
Reebok's first running shoe
ever was like the first running shoe

00:32:34.419 --> 00:32:38.156
and was it literally like nails
as track spikes?

00:32:38.156 --> 00:32:38.923
If you even think about

00:32:38.923 --> 00:32:43.328
like the first women's athletic shoe
was came out in the eighties,

00:32:43.328 --> 00:32:47.131
like designed specifically for women
and like that's not that long ago.

00:32:47.131 --> 00:32:50.868
You know it's like it's pretty crazy
to realize that we're in this moment.

00:32:50.868 --> 00:32:53.972
So I think that's what really gets me
excited about footwear.

00:32:53.972 --> 00:32:57.375
I love being able to contribute to that
in some way.

00:32:57.375 --> 00:33:00.511
And yeah,
just seeing brands that are coming up

00:33:00.511 --> 00:33:05.450
with really innovative things
and yeah, I just, I love that part of it.

00:33:05.950 --> 00:33:09.554
You are wearing a collaboration
that you've recently released

00:33:10.054 --> 00:33:12.357
with Reebok with your name on it.

00:33:12.557 --> 00:33:15.393
This was a huge full circle moment for me

00:33:15.393 --> 00:33:19.330
from being a literal intern
at the brand to having a shoe.

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:22.800
I just never would have thought
in my wildest dreams

00:33:22.800 --> 00:33:26.304
the scared intern
that was afraid of everything.

00:33:27.138 --> 00:33:29.574
And to now they came to me

00:33:29.574 --> 00:33:32.610
with the model
and it was a women's specific model.

00:33:32.910 --> 00:33:35.413
It's called the Go Mid
and I really loved it.

00:33:35.413 --> 00:33:37.882
I just thought it was customizable.

00:33:37.915 --> 00:33:40.018
There was a good canvas
for me to work off of

00:33:40.018 --> 00:33:43.321
and it reminded me of this shoe
from the nineties called the Aetna.

00:33:43.721 --> 00:33:47.825
And it's funny because that's really where
I pulled a lot of the inspiration from,

00:33:47.825 --> 00:33:51.129
and I got to utilize
my knowledge of the Reebok archive

00:33:51.129 --> 00:33:54.565
to be able to design my shoe
and also utilize

00:33:54.932 --> 00:33:58.836
my knowledge of the company
as a whole, like the development team

00:33:58.836 --> 00:34:01.172
and the marketing team
who I had worked with in the past.

00:34:01.172 --> 00:34:05.009
So it was like, we don't need to
be introduced because I know you already.

00:34:05.009 --> 00:34:06.377
And so it just worked really well.

00:34:06.377 --> 00:34:10.882
Having that experience and being able
to kind of put myself to the test.

00:34:12.183 --> 00:34:13.351
But yeah,

00:34:13.351 --> 00:34:16.354
my parents could not believe
that I had my name on a shoe.

00:34:16.354 --> 00:34:17.722
That was a really cool moment.

00:34:17.722 --> 00:34:20.358
They were like, You,
your name is on there. Like that's.

00:34:20.391 --> 00:34:21.592
Do they have pairs?

00:34:21.592 --> 00:34:22.026
They do.

00:34:22.026 --> 00:34:24.228
My mom is very she's sporting it.

00:34:24.228 --> 00:34:24.896
Yeah.

00:34:29.200 --> 00:34:31.702
What else is on the bucket list.

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:36.307
I definitely want to get more
into the homewares and furniture.

00:34:36.307 --> 00:34:40.778
That was something that I've, I've dabbled
in the past couple of years and ultimate

00:34:40.778 --> 00:34:45.016
dream of mine would be to have a kids
like a children's museum

00:34:45.016 --> 00:34:45.883
and have it

00:34:45.883 --> 00:34:50.121
be more of like a museum of science
or like an experimental type of place

00:34:50.121 --> 00:34:54.125
where people can come and touch and create
and make things so like

00:34:54.158 --> 00:34:57.361
almost like my workshops,
but in a larger sense

00:34:57.361 --> 00:34:59.664
and kid friendly.

00:35:00.398 --> 00:35:02.533
And also I have some

00:35:02.967 --> 00:35:06.104
really crazy projects coming up
that I'm excited about.

00:35:06.104 --> 00:35:10.875
And it is really it wasn't like dipping
a toe within the footwear industry.

00:35:10.875 --> 00:35:13.211
I'm like fully submerging myself in it.

00:35:13.211 --> 00:35:17.949
So I'm really excited to see how it goes
and also

00:35:18.049 --> 00:35:22.854
how every project we can do can be closer
to being a fully upcycled shoe.

00:35:23.154 --> 00:35:24.088
That's really the goal.

00:35:24.088 --> 00:35:28.092
That's honestly like within a five year
plan is to work with a brand, to do an

00:35:28.626 --> 00:35:33.431
entirely upcycled shoe at scale,
like not just 100 pairs,

00:35:33.431 --> 00:35:37.135
like a thousand
or a couple thousand plus pairs

00:35:37.135 --> 00:35:57.421
of shoes.

